            <div xml:id="d113">

                <pb facs="0181" n="1015" xml:id="pg1015"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112A/341</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-01-23T15:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 23, 1918, 3 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 8 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>8362. War Trade Board [from Frothingham]:</p>
                <p>No. 59. For Fuller. Foreign Trade Department suggests joint conference American,
                    British, and French Ministers, Minister of Persia, to agree upon list of Persian
                    enemy traders, American representative to be guided by your instructions in
                    accepting or declining names. They feel that intimate knowledge local conditions
                    essential and such local recommendations more effective than consideration of
                    evidence at long range. They further feel that moral effect of publication such
                    a Persian list by American Government is particularly desirable in supporting
                    previous efforts British and French authorities in Persia regardless of
                    negligible amount actual trade involved. If you approve proposal they will
                    instruct their Minister in Teheran accordingly. Aside from case of Persia,
                    Foreign Trade Department feels that this procedure might be desirable in
                    Morocco, Liberia, and certain other African territories, also for moral effect.
                    Await your views.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Page</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
